OpenAI's first AI intern is expected by the end of this year, but we got impatient and decided to build it ourselves. It runs autonomously for hours or days depending on the task, can read every paper, model, and dataset on the Hugging Face Hub to build the best post-training recipes, and works with any capable model (Kimi K2.6, GPT-5.4, Opus 4.7, etc.). It can run locally or on Hugging Face infrastructure, using Spaces as sandboxes, Jobs for generating data and training models, and Buckets for storage.

@akseljoonas: "Introducing ml-intern, the agent that just automated the post-training team at Hugging Face. It's an open-source implementation of the real research loop that our ML researchers do every day. You give it a prompt, it researches papers, goes through citations, implements ideas in GPU sandboxes, iterates, and builds deeply research-backed models for any use case. All built on the Hugging Face ecosystem. It can pull off crazy things: we made it train the best model for scientific reasoning, pushing the score from 10% to 32% on GPQA in under 10 hours. In healthcare settings, it generated 1100 synthetic data points from scratch and beat Codex on HealthBench by 60%. For competitive mathematics, it autonomously wrote a full GRPO script and ran ablations until it succeeded, all fully backed by papers."

ml-intern makes full use of the Hugging Face ecosystem by finding and reading papers on arXiv, walking citation graphs, pulling datasets, and launching training jobs on Hugging Face Jobs if no local GPUs are available. It embodies how researchers work and think, knowing how data should look and what good models feel like. It is being released today as a CLI and a web app for use on phones and desktops.

