ml-intern automates the post-training process by researching papers, going through citations, implementing ideas in GPU sandboxes, and building research-backed models. It has achieved impressive results, such as training a model for scientific reasoning that improved scores significantly in under 10 hours and generating synthetic data points for healthcare settings. It embodies how researchers work and think, making full use of the Hugging Face ecosystem.

@akseljoonas: "Introducing ml-intern, the agent that just automated the post-training team @huggingface. It's an open-source implementation of the real research loop that our ML researchers do every day."

I tested @huggingface ml-intern with the prompt to fine-tune a Segment Anything Model (SAM) on a useful medical dataset. It autonomously researched, found a dataset, wrote the finetuning script, trained the model, and created a comprehensive tutorial in a Jupyter Notebook file.

It researched via hf_papers, searched GitHub/HF Hub, trained using HF compute, pushed the weights, and wrote the article. Awesome stuff @akseljoonas, looking forward to using this.

@akseljoonas: "Introducing ml-intern, the agent that just automated the post-training team @huggingface. It's an open-source implementation of the real research loop that our ML researchers do every day. You give it a prompt, it researches papers, goes through citations, implements ideas in GPU sandboxes, iterates, and builds deeply research-backed models for any use case. All built on the Hugging Face ecosystem."

The ml-intern by @huggingface is impressive. You provide a high-level prompt, such as 'build the best scientific reasoning model' or 'crush healthcare benchmarks,' and this open-source agent handles the entire post-training loop.

It researches arXiv papers and citation graphs, pulls and cleans datasets from the HF Hub, implements SFT, GRPO, and synthetic data, launches training jobs (locally or on HF), monitors runs, reads evaluations, diagnoses failures, and iterates. Essentially, it acts like a full-time ML researcher that never sleeps and stays updated with live documentation.

@akseljoonas: "Introducing ml-intern, the agent that just automated the post-training team @huggingface. It's an open-source implementation of the real research loop that our ML researchers do every day. You give it a prompt, it researches papers, goes through citations, implements ideas in GPU sandboxes, iterates, and builds deeply research-backed models for any use case. All built on the Hugging Face ecosystem. It can pull off crazy things..."

We’ve post-trained a model on top of Qwen that achieves Pareto optimality on accuracy-cost curves. Unlike previous models, this one is designed to excel at both search and tool calls simultaneously, allowing us to unify the tool call router and summarization in one model.

The resulting model outperforms GPT and Sonnet in cost efficiency for serving daily Perplexity queries in production. The production model runs on our own inference platform, and we’re already handling a significant portion of our daily traffic with it, aiming to serve all default traffic soon. More research will follow on the models we’re training and deploying for Comet and Computer.

@perplexity_ai: "We've published new research on how we post-train models for accurate search-augmented answers. Our SFT + RL pipeline improves search, citation quality, instruction following, and efficiency. With Qwen models, we match or beat GPT models on factuality at a lower cost."

